  "body": "iter_phase_bundle_losses takes a generator so a caller can hold the bundle's sampling still, and every component honours it except one. get_dense_attachment_loss does not take a generator at all, and its three draws go to the process-global RNG: _sample_windings_by_circumference without the argument, torch.rand without it, and .uniform_ without it. The call site passes nothing either, three lines below get_min_spacing_loss, which is handed generator=generator.\r\n\r\nA caller that seeds a generator and hands it over therefore holds every component except attachment, which moves instead with whatever else on the process has drawn.\r\n\r\nWhat this is not, said here so it is not read wider than it is.\r\n\r\nIt is not a statement about training. The training step calls the bundle with no generator at all, so in training the global RNG is the run RNG: seeded from optimizer_random_seed, carried in the checkpoint as numpy_rng_state, torch_cpu_rng_state and torch_cuda_rng_states, restored on resume. Training is reproducible from its seed today and this does not change that.\r\n\r\nIt is not what keeps previews safe. export_preview saves numpy, torch CPU and torch CUDA state and restores all three in a finally. That envelope is what holds a preview off the training stream, not this argument.\r\n\r\nAnd the seam is not repository wide: losses.py does not contain the word generator once, with around twenty global draw sites. This closes the one hole inside the seam that does exist.\r\n\r\nWhere it bites. calibration/gradnorm.py exists to produce a weight for this component, and says so: \"Attachment is measured at weight 1.0 so a default can be chosen from the ratio.\" Its component loop seeds one generator per component, commented \"Fresh transform + identical seed per component\", and passes it to the bundle. Every component in that loop is held by that seed except dense_attachment, which samples from the global stream the loop itself advances. The one component the tool exists to calibrate is the one it cannot hold still, and the number moves when the number of earlier global draws changes while every other component's stays put. phase_tuning.py reports a pass to pass standard deviation off the same seam.\r\n\r\nThree lines: the parameter, keyword only, passed to the three draws, and given at the call site. The one existing positional caller in tests/test_sdt_losses.py is unaffected.\r\n\r\nThe test reuses sheet_volume, PerfectSpiralToX and spacing_cfg from tests/test_sdt_losses.py, so it adds no fixture files. Each case seeds an identical run generator twice and advances the global stream in between. min_spacing is the control and a determinism case guards the rest, so a difference cannot be mistaken for float noise. The fourth drives the real call site, and is the claim in one line: same bundle call, same generator, only the global stream differs.\r\n\r\n  the global stream moved these bundle components: ['dense_attachment'],\r\n  with the generator identical.\r\n  Untouched {'min_spacing': 0.002110552741214633,\r\n             'dense_attachment': 0.014046025462448597},\r\n  moved     {'min_spacing': 0.002110552741214633,\r\n             'dense_attachment': 0.0140679981559515}\r\n\r\nmin_spacing comes back bit identical; dense_attachment moves by 0.16 per cent.\r\n\r\n  PYTHONPATH=spiral-fitting python -m pytest -q \\\r\n    spiral-fitting/tests/test_attachment_generator.py\r\n\r\n  before  3 failed, 1 passed in 1.31s\r\n  after   4 passed in 1.33s\r\n\r\ntests/test_sdt_losses.py and tests/test_phase_spacing.py give 66 passed and 2 skipped both before and after.",
